            },
            "kind": "NO_CODE_CHANGE",
            "comments": [
                {
                    "file": "i2c-vr/xdpe1x2xx/xdpe1x2xx.cpp",
                    "line": 485,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Alexander",
                        "email": "alexander.hansen@9elements.com",
                        "username": "pointbazaar"
                    },
                    "message": "Hi, i made this example:\n\n```\nimage_size == 1000\nstart == 0\ni == 40\nimage[40] == `\\n`\n```\n\nThis will result in \n`line[i-start]= '\\0';`\nwhich is\n`line[40] = '\\0';`\nbut `line[39]` is the last element we can set.\n\nDo you agree that currently this code will fail if there is no newline encountered in the first 40 chars.\n\nWe get into this flow via\n`I2CVRDevice::updateDevice` -> `VoltageRegulator::verifyImage` -> `XDPE1X2XX::verifyImage` -> `XDPE1X2XX::parseImage`\n\nand i did not find a check which would prevent this issue in any of these functions.\n\nWould it work if we had something like\n```\nconst size_t lineLength = i - start;\nif (lineLength >= maxLineLength) {\n    error(\"line length exceeded\");\n    return false;\n}\nstd::memcpy(line, image + start, lineLength);\nline[lineLength] = '\\0';\n```"
                },
                {
                    "file": "i2c-vr/xdpe1x2xx/xdpe1x2xx.cpp",
                    "line": 485,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Christopher Meis",
                        "email": "christopher.meis@9elements.com",
                        "username": "ChriMarMe"
                    },
                    "message": "Not sure if that should be the solution. The issue is the lack of \\n in the line variable. I think if you adapt the memcpy to:\n`std::memcpy(line, image + start, i - start + 1);`\nit should work without the bogus logs."
                },
                {
                    "file": "i2c-vr/xdpe1x2xx/xdpe1x2xx.cpp",
                    "line": 485,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Christopher Meis",
                        "email": "christopher.meis@9elements.com",
                        "username": "ChriMarMe"
                    },
                    "message": "Also line length should be 36 (3 + 4 * 8 byte according to programming guide + 1 for \\n)"
                },
                {
                    "file": "i2c-vr/xdpe1x2xx/xdpe1x2xx.cpp",
                    "line": 485,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Christopher Meis",
                        "email": "christopher.meis@9elements.com",
                        "username": "ChriMarMe"
                    },
                    "message": "oh, i forgot the blanks, which makes it 40 + 1"
                },
                {
                    "file": "i2c-vr/xdpe1x2xx/xdpe1x2xx.cpp",
                    "line": 485,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "LeoYangQuanta",
                        "email": "Leo-Yang@quantatw.com",
                        "username": "LeoYangQuanta"
                    },
                    "message": "Hi Alexander,\nCurrently, the maximum length of a line containing \\n in image files is only 40 characters.\nTherefore, I believe this edge case should not occur at present, but implementing additional checks would indeed provide greater safety.\nThanks.\n\n\nHi Christopher,\nI think this problem should come from that the input lg2 uses const char*, string_view will rely on '\\0' to calculate the length when it is constructed from const char*\nDue to the lack of '\\0' in the copying, the length may crosses to the outside of the buffer incorrectly and causes overflow.\nThanks."
                },
                {
                    "file": "i2c-vr/xdpe1x2xx/xdpe1x2xx.cpp",
                    "line": 709,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Alexander",
                        "email": "alexander.hansen@9elements.com",
                        "username": "pointbazaar"
                    },
                    "message": "Agree with this fix, looks good to me."
                }
            ],
